<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrea del Sarto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1486">1486</a>–<a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Andrea d'Agnolo di Francesco di Luca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrea del Sarto was a Florentine painter of the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and early Mannerism, known for his frescoes, altarpieces, portraits, drawing and colour; his name came from his father's trade as a tailor. From 1509 to 1514 he painted frescoes at the Santissima Annunziata in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and he worked for years on a series in the cloister of the Scalzo. At the invitation of François I he went to the French court in 1518 but soon returned to Florence. His works include the Madonna of the Harpies of 1517 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a> at San Salvi. Called a painter without errors in his lifetime, he died of plague in Florence in 1530.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrea-del-sarto.jpg" alt="Andrea del Sarto" width="330" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea del Sarto · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarto,_Andrea_del_-_Self-portrait_-_Uffizi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrea was born Andrea d'Agnolo di Francesco di Luca in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> on 16 July 1486. His father, Agnolo, was a tailor, whence the name del Sarto, 'tailor's son'. By 1494 he was apprenticed to a goldsmith and then to a woodcarver and painter named Gian Barile, with whom he stayed until 1498. According to Vasari he later apprenticed to Piero di Cosimo and Raffaellino del Garbo. With an older friend, Franciabigio, he opened a joint studio in the Piazza del Grano. The Encyclopædia Britannica describes his style as marked by an exceptional interest in colour and atmosphere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1509 to 1514 the Servite Order employed him in frescoes at the Santissima Annunziata. He completed seven frescoes in the forecourt, five of them on the life and miracles of the Servite Filippo Benizzi. He declined a second cycle on Saint Sebastian, probably because of low pay, and painted instead a Procession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a>, finished in 1511 with a self-portrait, and a Nativity of the Virgin of 1514. These won him the nickname Andrea senza errori, 'Andrea without fault'. His last work at the Scalzo, the Nativity of the Baptist, came in 1526.</p><p>A Pietà and a Madonna sent to the French court led to an invitation from François I in 1518. Vasari said that Andrea spent money the king gave him for buying art on a house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>; some historians dismiss the story as apocryphal. In 1523 he copied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>'s portrait group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> so faithfully that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giulio-romano">Giulio Romano</a> was fooled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He had married Lucrezia del Fede, widow of a hatter, on 26 December 1512; she appears in many of his paintings, often as a Madonna. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> aged 44 during an outbreak of bubonic plague at the end of September 1530. His renown was eclipsed after his death by that of Leonardo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>, and Vasari, his pupil, thought him to lack the divine fire of Leonardo, Michelangelo and Raphael. Jacopo Pontormo, Rosso Fiorentino and Francesco Salviati were among those who first followed his style.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrea del Sarto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
